                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "If you loved the first game, Chaos on Deponia brings back the gorgeous hand-drawn art, sharp wit, and wacky humor you expect. The concept of Goal's mind splitting into three distinct personalities (Lady, Spunky, and Baby Goal) is brilliant on paper and leads to some hilarious banter\r\n\r\nHowever, the game suffers from severe \"middle-episode syndrome\"\r\n\r\n• Pacing Issues: The \"Three-Goal Marathon\" takes up nearly 70% of the game. Persuading all three personalities to return to Doc turns the middle act into a bloated, padded checklist that grinds the plot to a halt\r\n\r\n• Classic \"Daedalic Moon Logic\": Unless you are playing Harvey's New Eyes, Daedalic just loves obtuse puzzles. You will absolutely hit points where logic goes out the window, forcing you to use a guide or randomly rub inventory trash against every pixel on the screen\r\n\r\n• Convoluted Plot & Lore Holes: By the final act, the narrative buckles under its own weight. The franchise's macro-plot starts making zero sense (if Elysium is blowing up Deponia anyway, what was the point of the expedition in game one?). To top it off, the final climax devolves into an incredibly messy, confusing shell game of swapping remotes, cartridges, and bodies",
